NAME
    context-run - execute one command inside a selected m context

SYNOPSIS
    context-run <context-selector>... -- <command> [<argument> ...]
    context-run --resolve <facility> <constraint>...

DESCRIPTION
    The caller enters through m: m context-run ...
    The execution form applies ordered context selectors within this child
    process only, then re-enters the m bootstrap with a validated context envelope before\n    execing the requested command, so selected system global environment\n    snapshots are applied. The parent shell and its
    context are not modified. Selectors use the native context syntax:
    pkg=..., system=..., user=..., locale=..., osarch=...,
    provider.<facility>=..., full-context=<preset>.

    No package installation or persistent context configuration occurs.
    Errors in selector validation prevent execution. Arguments are passed
    without shell interpretation, and exec preserves signal/exit behavior.

    --resolve checks one facility requirement against the selected context.
    A pinned provider.<facility> is validated against the supplied constraints;
    without a pin, the existing pkg system-default requirement resolution is
    used for compatibility. The command prints the concrete provider on
    success and never installs or binds packages.

    --resolve uses the active context and does not accept selectors itself.
    To resolve in another context, run context-run <selectors> --
    context-run --resolve <facility> <constraint>... .

EXAMPLES
    m context-run pkg=nodejs@v26.8.2 -- node --version
    m context-run full-context=development -- node app.js
    m context-run provider.node=nodejs@v26.8.2 -- context-run --resolve node '>=26'

EXIT STATUS
    Command mode: the executed command's status (or context failure).
    Requirement mode: 0 on success, 1 on unavailable/incompatible provider,
    2 on invalid invocation.

OWNERSHIP
    Context selects installed packages and validated providers in m-os.
    pkg supplies facility metadata and compatibility checking.
    Development tools such as mk delegate to this boundary; they do not
    recreate context resolution or PATH construction.
